2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_5064.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>Parshwanath 2 of 108 - Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: In the temple (derasar) located at Gopipura, Subhash Chowk, Surat, Gujarat, the Main Deity is Lord Shri Shitalnath. In the underground shrine (bhoyaru) there is an idol of Lord Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanath in white stone, in the standing kaussagga posture, 57 inches tall and 17 inches wide. Above the head of the idol is a canopy of 1,000 serpent hoods (nagaphana). The parikar (surrounding frame) is carved with the ten gandharvas of Lord Parshwanath. Near the feet of the Lord is an idol of Padmavati. Meghmali is depicted bowing at the feet of Lord Parshwanath, seated there and begging forgiveness for his misdeeds. This is a unique, artistic and captivating idol carved from a single block of stone - the only one of its kind.</p><p>Location: This temple is in Gopipura and Subhash Chowk in Surat.</p><p>History: Surat is also known as &quot;Sone ki Moorat&quot; (the golden image/city). Surat is an ancient city with several famous old Jain temples; a very large Jain population resides here, many of them very prosperous. Shreshthi Shri Bhaidas Nemidas was one such person. After listening to the religious discourses of Shri Jinalabhsurishwarji, this shreshthi felt he must do something for the Jain community. So he had a grand, striking and beautiful temple built, and on the 12th day of the bright half of Vaishakh in Vikram Samvat 1827, the anjanshalaka (consecration) of the idol of Lord Shri Shitalnath as Main Deity, together with idols of 181 Tirthankaras, was performed and installed by the revered hands of Acharya Shri Jinalabhsurishwarji. In the basement (tahkhana) of this temple, Lord Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanath was installed as Main Deity in Vikram Samvat 1828. In Vikram Samvat 1829, this shreshthi built a beautiful temple on the hills of Shri Shatrunjay, which still stands today. This shreshthi also built the Shri Shitalvadi Upashraya in the Oswal locality of Surat. For his religious activities, he was given the title &quot;Ashadhi Megh.&quot; This idol of Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanath is very impressive, unique, miraculous and captivating. The ascetic Maharaj Saheb Shri Jaysomvijayji, under the guidance of Acharya Shri Vijay Bhuvanbhanusurishwarji, completed a long fast of 68 days before this idol smoothly and without any difficulty. It is said that no devotee can worship this idol continuously for 6 months. Miracles keep occurring at this temple - once, during the worship of the 108 Parshwanath idols, saffron-colored ami (nectar) was seen on the idol, and devotees have on many occasions had the vision (darshan) of Dharnendra Dev (the serpent deity) at the temple.</p><p>The Mystery of the Idol: Once, when Lord Parshwanath was standing in kaussagga meditation, Meghmali brought down a torrential downpour upon him. The water rose to the Lord&#x27;s nose, but the Lord continued chanting the Navkar Mahamantra in the kaussagga posture. In an earlier life, the Lord had rescued a snake and a she-snake being burned in a fire lit by Kamath, and had given them a good rebirth by reciting the Navkar Mahamantra to them; they were then born in the heavens as the goddess Padmavati and the god Dharnendra. When they recall the affliction inflicted upon the Lord, Padmavati Devi lifts up her throne so that the Lord does not drown, and Dharnendra Dev forms a canopy over the Lord&#x27;s head to stop the rain. Even after this ordeal, Meghmali sees that the Lord&#x27;s kaussagga posture has not wavered even slightly, and he realizes that this is Lord Parsha; he repents, falls at the Lord&#x27;s feet and begs forgiveness. In this idol, the Lord is shown in the kaussagga posture, Padmavati Devi is shown holding up the throne, Meghmali is shown begging forgiveness at his feet, and Dharnendra has formed the canopy of a thousand serpent hoods. Just as a lotus flower does not sink in water, symbolizing this, the Lord&#x27;s idol is set upon a lotus throne. On either side of the parikar are five small idols each, representing the Lord&#x27;s first ten disciples (Ganadharas). This idol, carved from a single stone, is considered miraculous.</p><p>Other Temples: Surat has many ancient temples. There are 5 temples in Gopipura - Agam Mandir, Ashtapadji Mandir, Surajmandan Parshwanath Mandir, Umarwadi Parshwanath Mandir and Shri Vasupujya Swami Mandir.</p><p>Art and Sculpture: This idol is the only one of its kind in the world - carved from a single stone, in the kaussagga posture, with the hands shown resting on a lotus flower; the canopy of serpent hoods has, in each of its first 9 hoods, two eyes, and all 1,000 serpent hoods are finely carved.</p><p>Directions: Surat&#x27;s railway station is on the Ahmedabad-Mumbai railway line. Bus and private vehicle services are available. Facilities here include an upashraya, pathshalas, a knowledge repository (gyan bhandar), an ayambilshala, a dharmashala and a bhojanshala.</p><p>Scripture References: Sahastraphana Parshwanath is mentioned in texts such as &quot;365 Shri Parshwanath Jin Naammala,&quot; &quot;108 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Sankhya Stavan,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Naammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Prabhu ke 108 Naam ke Chhand,&quot; &quot;135 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; and others. In &quot;Shri Ratnasar, Part 2,&quot; Shri Mohanlalji wrote about the good deeds of Shreshthi Shri Bhaidas. Besides Surat Gopipura, idols of Sahastraphana Parshwanath are also found at Jiravala Tirth, Bhildiyaji Tirth, Kavi Tirth, the Kalikund Parshwanath Mandir in Santacruz Mumbai, Babulnath Road Mandir Mumbai, Adinath Mandir Mumbai, and in Pune, Ahmedabad, Patan, Radhanpur, Palitana, Udaipur, Jodhpur, Girnar, Ranakpur, Madhuban Shikharji, Kolkata, Ahmednagar, and elsewhere.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Arham Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Shitalnath Jain Derasar, Shri Sahastranath Parshwanath Jain Derasar, Gopipura, Subhash Chowk, Surat - 395001, Gujarat, India. Phone: 0261-2595279, Mobile: 09375277873.</p><p>Parshwanath 3 of 108 - Shri Surajmandan Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: In the Hathiwala Derasar temple, the Main Deity is Lord Shri Dharmanath. In the basement (bhoyaru) of this temple is an idol of Shri Surajmandan Parshwanath in white stone, in the padmasana (seated) posture, 61 inches tall and 45 inches wide, with 9 serpent hoods. Above the head of the idol is a canopy of 9 serpent hoods. This idol is in the basement of the Shri Dharmanath temple, which is known as the &quot;Hathiwala Mandir&quot; (Elephant Temple).</p><p>Location: This is in Gopipura, Surat.</p><p>History: Surat is a very famous city of Gujarat, renowned worldwide for its diamond industry and sarees. On the 5th Thursday of the dark half of Kartik in Vikram Samvat 1679, under the Punarvasu nakshatra, this idol of Shri Surajmandan Parshwanath was installed by the revered hands of Shri Ratnachandra Gani, disciple of Shri Shantichandra Upadhyay, who had brought about a transformation in the life of Emperor Akbar. This beautiful idol of Lord Parshwanath was installed with Shreshthi Gopidas, and so this area came to be known as &quot;Gopipura&quot; after Shreshthi Gopidas. On the 5th Monday of the dark half of the month of Jeth in Vikram Samvat 1664, the idol of Lord Shri Dharmanath was installed by the revered hands of Acharya Shri Hirvijaysurishwarji. This temple is also known as the &quot;Hathiwala Mandir.&quot; Shri Vastupal Omji built a guru temple here called &quot;Hir Vihar&quot; and installed the footprints (padukas) of Shri Hirvijaysurishwarji and Shri Sensurji. This idol of Surajmandan Parshwanath is miraculous, impressive, striking, magnificent, and always appears to be smiling. Sheth Shri Shantidas worshipped the &quot;Shri Chintamani Mantra&quot; before this idol. This derasar has a rangmandap measuring 55x55 feet, said to be the largest of its kind found anywhere in India, featuring exquisite carving including the 16 Vidyadevis, panels of ashoka trees, hundreds of serpent hoods, over 100 elephants, the 24 Tirthankaras with their yakshas and yakshinis, and domes with 170 Tirthankaras and deities along with the ashtamangal symbols and their weapons. This place of pilgrimage also has a miniature replica of Palitana.</p><p>Other Temples: In Gopipura, Surat, there are the temples of Shri Vasupujya Swami, Agam Mandir, Ashtapadji Mandir, Shri Umarwadi Parshwanath Mandir and Shri Sahastraphana Parshwanath Mandir. Surat has 5 (five) of the 108 Parshwanath shrines.</p><p>Directions: Surat&#x27;s railway station is on the Ahmedabad-Mumbai railway line. Facilities here include an upashraya, pathshalas, a gyan bhandar, an ayambilshala, a dharmashala and a bhojanshala.</p><p>Scripture References: Surajmandan Parshwanath is mentioned in &quot;Shri Parshwanath Naammala,&quot; &quot;Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Chaitya Paripati,&quot; &quot;Shri Vinaysuri Ratnaras,&quot; &quot;Chovisi,&quot; &quot;Surat ki Gazal,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwa Prabhu ke 108 Naam ka Chhand,&quot; &quot;Shri Jin Sahastra Naam Stotra,&quot; and other texts. Upadhyay Shri Vinayvijayji called Surajmandan Parshwanath &quot;Diggaj Parshwanath.&quot; Idols of Surajmandan Parshwanath are also found at Shirsala in Maharashtra, at the Kalikund Parshwanath Mandir in Santacruz, Mumbai, and at Jiravala Tirth.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Arham Shri Surajmandan Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Surajmandan Parshwanath Jain Mandir, Hathi Vali Gali, Gopipura, Surat - 395001, Gujarat, India. Phone: 0261-2591333, Mobile: 098795-16442.</p><p>Parshwanath 4 of 108 - Shri Vighnahara Parshwanath</p><p>Main Deity: An idol of Lord Shri Vighnahara Parshwanath in black stone, in the padmasana posture, 41 inches tall and 27 inches wide. Above the head of the idol is a canopy of hundreds of serpent hoods (sahastraphana).</p><p>Location: This is at Nishal Sheri in Rander, near Surat.</p><p>History: Rander is an ancient village near Surat. This temple was built by the Jain Sangh around Vikram Samvat 1800, with the idol of Lord Adinath installed as Main Deity. The idol bears the inscription &quot;Vikram Samvat 1683.&quot; The idol of Lord Vighnahara Parshwanath stands beside that of Lord Adinath. It is believed that this idol of Lord Parshwanath is more than 300 years old. At one time, Rander had 14 Jain temples and 143 Jain idols. While writing &quot;Shri Shripal Ras&quot; during his chaturmas (four-month monsoon retreat) in Rander in Vikram Samvat 1638, Mahopadhyay Shri Vinayvijayji attained the lotus feet of the Lord; at his indication, &quot;Shri Shripal Ras&quot; was completed here by Mahopadhyay Shri Yashovijayji.</p><p>Other Temples: At present, Rander has 5 temples; the temple of Lord Neminath here is very ancient.</p><p>Directions: Rander is situated on the bank of a river, 16 km from Surat. The nearest railway station is Surat. Bus and private vehicle services are available from Surat to Rander. Rander has an upashraya and a dharmashala.</p><p>Scripture References: Vighnahara Parshwanath is mentioned in &quot;Shri Suryapur Chaitya Paripati,&quot; &quot;108 Naam Garbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,&quot; &quot;365 Shri Parshwajin Naammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Parshwanath Naammala,&quot; &quot;Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;Surat Chaitya Paripati,&quot; and others. The main temple of Vighnahara Parshwanath is in Rander. There is also a temple of Vighnahara Parshwanath near Mandsaur and at Devgadh near Udaipur. In Mumbai, at Walkeshwar, Babeshwar (Babu Kat Mandir), there is an idol in a beautiful parikar with the ashtamangal symbols, the nine planets (navagraha) and the ten guardian deities of directions (dasdigpal). Vighnahara Parshwanath has striking idols at Antariksha Tirth and at many other temples as well.</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hreem Arham Shri Vighnahara Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Vighnahara Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Shri Adi-Neminath Jain Derasar Pedhi, Uttamram Street, Nisha Faliya, Post: Rander, Surat - 395005, Gujarat, India. Phone: 0261-2760149, Mobile: 9925198118.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-001\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-x